\[
eV_s = \frac{hc}{\lambda} - \phi
\]  
So the change in stopping voltage when wavelength changes:  
\[
\Delta V_s = \frac{hc}{e} \left( \frac{1}{\lambda_2} - \frac{1}{\lambda_1} \right)
\]  
Given \(\lambda_1 = 400\ \text{nm}\), \(\lambda_2 = 300\ \text{nm}\):  

\[
\frac{1}{\lambda_2} - \frac{1}{\lambda_1} = \frac{1}{300\times10^{-9}} - \frac{1}{400\times10^{-9}}
= \frac{10^9}{300} - \frac{10^9}{400} 
= 10^9 \left(  \frac{4 - 3}{1200} \right)
= \frac{10^9}{1200}
\]  

Now:  
\[
\frac{hc}{e} = \frac{6.63\times10^{-34} \times 3\times10^8}{1.60\times10^{-19}}
= \frac{1.989\times10^{-25}}{1.60\times10^{-19}} 
= 1.243125 \ \text{V·nm? Actually, let's compute precisely:}
\]  
\[
\frac{hc}{e} = \frac{6.63\times10^{-34} \times 3\times10^8}{1.6\times10^{-19}}
= \frac{1.989\times10^{-25}}{1.6\times10^{-19}} 
= 1.243125\times10^{-6}\ \text{V·m}
= 1243.125\ \text{V·nm}
\]  
Thus:  
\[
\Delta V_s = 1243.125 \times \frac{1}{1200} \ \text{V} \approx 1.0359\ \text{V}
\]  

